{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1339,
    "dDurationMs": 2121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When things move, they create waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3460,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if you shake a stick back and\nforth in water: water waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8100,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Vibrate a piece of metal back and forth really\nfast: air pressure waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11620,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shake some electrons back and forth really\nfast: radio waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15300,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yes, shake a planet or star back and forth\nreally fast: gravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20980,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravitational waves happen because the effects\nof gravity don't travel outwards at infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24789,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed – so if the sun were to suddenly jump\na few hundred thousand kilometers to the side,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29510,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the changed gravitational field would take\ntime to pulse outwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32940,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if the sun shook back and forth and back\nand forth, instead of a single pulse, you'd"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36730,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get continuous gravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39150,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what's doing the \"waving\"?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40680,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of water, the height of the water\nincreases and decreases at any particular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45120,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "location as the waves travel past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47010,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of sound, the pressure of the\nair increases and decreases at any particular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51540,
    "dDurationMs": 1929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "location as the waves travel past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53469,
    "dDurationMs": 4511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of radio or cell phone signals\nor any other electromagnetic waves, the electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57980,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and magnetic fields get stronger and weaker\nat any particular location as the waves travel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62020,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63020,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in the case of gravitational waves, the\ngravitational field gets slightly stronger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66460,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or weaker as the waves travel past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68750,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can tell a wave has passed by looking\nat how nearby particles behave – a bobber"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72729,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the water rises up and down, the electrons\nin a radio antenna move back and forth because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77049,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the changing electric field, and free-floating\npeople or planets or cats move back and forth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81549,
    "dDurationMs": 4061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because of the changing gravitational field\n– though in this last case, the peculiarities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85610,
    "dDurationMs": 4099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity mean that the free-floating things\nactually experiencing the gravitational wave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89709,
    "dDurationMs": 1381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don't feel like they're moving."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91090,
    "dDurationMs": 3759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you measure the space between them\nby sending a pulse of laser light and measuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94849,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the time it takes for it to come back, you'll\nfind that the distance between them increases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98469,
    "dDurationMs": 1421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and decreases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99890,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In practice, physicists don't actually measure\ngravitational waves with free-floating cats"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103999,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– they use very very fancy expensive mirrors\nwhich are effectively free-floating because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109369,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're hung on pendulums suspended on isolation\ntables suspended on isolation tables, or which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113630,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are _actually_ free-floating because they're\nattached to satellites floating in space – though"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117700,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this hasn't been done yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119000,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason physicists need fancy floating\nmirrors to detect gravitational waves is that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122979,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the waves are very, very weak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125259,
    "dDurationMs": 4041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the electrons that vibrate back\nand forth in a radio antenna to make electromagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129300,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves ALSO make gravitational waves; electrons\n_are_ matter moving back and forth after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134430,
    "dDurationMs": 4889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the waves they make are super weak: a\n200 watt radio transmitter gives off something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139319,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a quadrillionth of a quintillionth of\na quintillionth of a quintillionth of that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143329,
    "dDurationMs": 2281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power as gravitational radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145610,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's why here on earth we can only detect\nthe biggest baddest astronomical events – like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150659,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superfast spinning neutron stars or merging\nblack holes or the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154799,
    "dDurationMs": 43871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Though so far, we've only detected black hole\ncollisions."
    } ]
  } ]
}
